    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47158.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "And there's, there's enough of them. Yes, that it's a, it's a national issue. So let's, let's talk about that for a moment. I'm my, my guest yesterday was a Fargo defense attorney Mark Fries and some of the things that we talked about were the sort of policies that we could put in place to address this. And part of his, he had a whole list of issues and a lot of it has to do with militarization. We need more training. We need to get rid of some of the warrior cop mentality that is prevalent in some areas of law enforcement. One thing we talked about was qualified immunity. Now, I know one of your colleagues in Congress, Justin Amash, Congressman Amash, he's pushing, he's announced policy like this. I haven't looked at it. It's a little bit of a convoluted issue. Honestly, it's hard for me to understand this precedent that the courts have said. The way Mark explained it to me, and you're a lawyer like Mark, so I'm a Yahoo blogger, but I'm gonna do my best with this. The way he explained it is the courts have said, The police have immunity unless you can come in and prove that you have standing for your rights to be violated. And to prove that, there has to be some previous case that is very specific and very like the claim that you're making. So I saw somebody, I was reading this USA Today article, and somebody said that in order for George Floyd's family to be successful in a civil suit, they would have to come in and prove with a previous case that for nine minutes or whatever it was, an officer kneeling on your neck for nine minutes, you can't breathe, violate your rights. And you have to prove it like that's sort of the standard, which if I'm describing that right, just seems absolutely ridiculous to me.",
