  {
    "episode_id": 47158,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47158.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "That's exactly right. And it's a judge made law and it really should be looked at and reformed. I'm not entirely sure where, I mean, as far as abolishing it because I don't want to open up every law enforcement officer to a lawsuit under any circumstances. I also, I mean, I will just say this. And this is from years of dealing with this. We're going to have a hearing next week on police brutality. And before we start, I mean, there's some reforms we can probably do and we should look at But before we start doing national one size fits all, that actually makes our cops less safe. There's something that I think is pretty true. Good cops know who bad cops are. And we need to figure out a way to incentivize departments for weeding out bad cops. And we also need to make sure that when they do, they don't just get the hop to another department. Because I think you'll find that they exist, but there aren't Nearly as many of them as people would like you to believe. The problem is this, I mean, this internal review process where nobody, I mean, no sunshine's ever shined on it and nobody really knows it occurs.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The current legal framework is judge-made law.",
