  {
    "episode_id": 47158,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/7/47158.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 65,
    "turn_text": "I was just going to say, and that's exactly right. And, I mean, a lot of these reforms, I mean, you know, whether it was weight of crack cocaine versus regular cocaine, I mean, those laws were specifically targeted to a minority group and we've done good. That doesn't mean we don't have a lot more to do and we do. But I think the fundamental understanding of all of these issues needs to be that we can do a lot of reform at the federal level. What we have to do is incentivize states to follow that lead because 97 percent of law enforcement in this country occurs at the state and local level and we still want to maintain that and we still want to maintain local control. These reforms are great, and they are good, and there's more to do. But we have to ensure that the policies we are enacting at the federal level one, don't trample on states and local rights, and two, figure out a way to push that. That's where the federal government can really help right now. Push that conversation locally as well.",
